-- erwartet: M101
-- **A sixteen-bit value stored into a BYTE through a pointer -- accepted, and the C truncated
-- it.**
--
-- This is the write half of `gift/1367`, and it is the half that costs something. Measured on
-- the release binary of `f55b4713`, the commit before this one:
--
-- ```text
-- impl fn kaputt(buf : ptr<normal, w> u8, wert : u64 in 0 .. 65535)
--     requires 1 <= lenof(buf) effects { writes buf }
-- { buf[0] = wert; }              -- 0 errors, 0 hints
-- ```
--
-- `m1.bereich`'s own sentence says *"no overflow, no truncating width change … reaches the
-- emitter without a run-time check being emitted for it."* **This was a truncating width
-- change, and it reached the emitter with nothing at all**: the assignment target had no type,
-- because `typ_von_ort` answered `Unbekannt` for an index on a pointer (the reason is in
-- `gift/1367`'s header), and `Unbekannt` is compatible with everything. The generated C is
-- `buf[0] = wert;` over a `uint8_t *`, which C converts silently -- `wert = 0x0102` stores
-- `0x02` and nobody says a word.
--
-- *A checker that computes an interval for every value has to have a value to compute it for.*
-- The refusal names the remedy it always names: `narrow wert to 0 .. 255 else { … }`, or a
-- declaration that carries the range -- and then the truncation is written, not suffered.
-- `beispiele/168` is the form a network stack actually wants: the two bytes of a big-endian
-- 16-bit field, each of them provably a byte.

module gift {

pub impl fn kaputt(buf : ptr<normal, w> u8, wert : u64 in 0 .. 65535)
    requires 1 <= lenof(buf)
    effects  { writes buf }
    costs    <= 8 ops
{
    buf[0] = wert;
}

}
